# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Convention used in this file: a variable that is COMMENTED OUT is read by no
# code in this repo today. Each one is kept, with a note, because it names
# something the design expects to need later — not because setting it does
# anything now. Uncommenting one has no effect until the logic behind it is
# built, and the note says what that logic would be. Everything left uncommented
# IS read somewhere, and the comment above it says where.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# XRPL Configuration — NOT WIRED UP. Nothing reads these three. Every component
# takes its endpoint from the wallets file/secret instead: functions/shared.py:52
# and src/mcp_server/server.py read `_metadata.rpc_url` out of the wallet JSON
# and fall back to a hardcoded testnet URL. Editing XRPL_RPC_URL here repoints
# nothing.
#
# When you would need them: the day this runs against anything other than
# testnet. Network choice then has to come from configuration rather than from a
# field inside the wallet file plus a hardcoded default — switching to mainnet is
# exactly the change that must not depend on someone remembering to regenerate
# wallets.json. That resolution logic is not built yet.
# XRPL_NETWORK=testnet
# XRPL_RPC_URL=https://s.altnet.rippletest.net:51234
# XRPL_WSS_URL=wss://s.altnet.rippletest.net:51233

# AWS Configuration
AWS_REGION=us-west-2
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=us-west-2

# NOT WIRED UP. src/signing/kms_signer.py:253 does read this, but nothing in the
# repo imports that module, and its KMS signing path raises NotImplementedError
# (kms_signer.py:219). Setting it changes nothing today.
#
# When you would need it: replacing seed-based signing with a KMS key that can
# sign but never export — the T2 target on architecture slide 1, and the
# prerequisite for this handling anything of real value. The signing logic is not
# built yet; src/signing/kms_signer.py is the scaffold.
# KMS_KEY_ID=

# Web app login — no default, the app refuses to start without these.
# In AWS, source the password from Secrets Manager rather than a plaintext env var.
XRPL_AGENTIC_USERNAME=
XRPL_AGENTIC_PASSWORD=

# Origins allowed to open the /ws/chat and /ws/metrics WebSockets, comma
# separated (e.g. https://payments.example.com). A browser will not let script
# forge the Origin header, so this is what stops a page on another site from
# opening a payment socket with your cookie attached. Leave blank to allow only
# the app's own host, taken from the Host header.
XRPL_AGENTIC_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Set to 1 ONLY to run against http://localhost: it drops Secure from the
# session cookie and disables the HTTP→HTTPS redirect. Never set in a
# deployment — the session cookie authorises payments.
XRPL_AGENTIC_INSECURE_COOKIES=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AgentCore — every value below is an output of YOUR deployment. They are
# generated per-deployment, so there are no meaningful defaults; fill them in
# after deploying (see DESIGN.md and scripts/setup_agentcore_payments.py).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The Gateway is the tool path: it fronts the eight tool Lambdas, one target
# each, and src/agentcore_client.py POSTs SigV4-signed MCP calls to this URL.
# Required — without it no tool call is possible, and there is deliberately no
# fallback that would bypass the Gateway. It is the ToolsStack's GatewayUrl
# output; the EKS deployment sets it for you.
#   aws cloudformation describe-stacks --stack-name XrplAgenticPaymentsToolsStack \
#     --query "Stacks[0].Outputs[?OutputKey=='GatewayUrl'].OutputValue" --output text
AGENTCORE_GATEWAY_URL=

# Gateway identifier. Only the target health check and the web UI's
# infrastructure panel read this; tool calls do not need it.
AGENTCORE_GATEWAY_ID=

# MCP protocol version to speak to the Gateway. Must be one the Gateway
# accepts, so leave it unset unless you also changed MCP_PROTOCOL_VERSION in
# infra/lib/tools-stack.ts — that constant pins both sides.
AGENTCORE_MCP_PROTOCOL_VERSION=

# Runtime hosting the MCP server — a second implementation of the same eight
# tools, and NOT DEPLOYED: nothing in the repo creates a Runtime, so there is no
# value to fill in here unless you created one by hand. Not read by
# invoke_tool(); only the web UI's infrastructure panel reads these, and unset it
# reports "not deployed". Leaving them blank costs you nothing.
AGENTCORE_RUNTIME_ARN=
AGENTCORE_RUNTIME_ID=

# CloudWatch log group for the Runtime, used by the observability tab.
AGENTCORE_RUNTIME_LOG_GROUP=

# AgentCore Payments (x402 micropayments on premium tools). These three are read
# by src/agentcore_client.py. Left unset, the x402 charge degrades to a logged
# "simulated" receipt rather than failing the tool call, so a run without them
# still works end to end — it just does not settle the per-call charge.
PAYMENT_MANAGER_ARN=
PAYMENT_MANAGER_ID=
PAYMENT_CONNECTOR_ID=

# NOT WIRED UP. scripts/setup_agentcore_payments.py creates the credential
# provider and records its ARN in config/payments.json, but no code reads that
# file or this variable — the payment call reaches Coinbase through
# PAYMENT_CONNECTOR_ID above. (Note that config/payments.json being inert means
# running the setup script is not sufficient: copy its output into the three
# variables above by hand.)
#
# When you would need it: rotating or swapping the Coinbase credential provider
# without re-running the setup script, or pointing at a provider created outside
# this repo. That lookup is not built yet.
# CREDENTIAL_PROVIDER_ARN=

# Managed Harness. NOT READ BY ANY CODE — recorded here because DESIGN.md's
# migration table names it. The live implementation is the five-agent
# orchestrator in src/agents/orchestrator.py, and agentcore.json has
# "harnesses": [], so nothing switches to InvokeHarness today.
AGENTCORE_HARNESS_ARN=

# No COGNITO_* variables are listed here on purpose. Cognito JWT auth is target
# T3 on slide 1 and nothing in this repo implements it: infra/ provisions no user
# pool, and the deployed FastAPI app authenticates with a signed session cookie
# against the credentials in xrpl-agentic-payments/web-auth instead. Listing the
# variable names would imply a code path that would read them.

# NOT WIRED UP as a variable — though the secret it names is real and required.
# The tool Lambdas load `xrpl-agentic-payments/wallets` at cold start
# (functions/shared.py:56) but hardcode the id, so nothing reads this. Wallet
# material is not bundled into container images or the AgentCore archive, and is
# not mounted into the web pod either: infra/lib/eks-stack.ts defines no wallets
# SecretProviderClass. Dockerfile.webapp sets the same value as documentation.
#
# When you would need it: running more than one wallet set — a second
# environment, or a rotation that lands the seeds under a new secret name — at
# which point the hardcoded id in functions/shared.py has to become a lookup.
# That indirection is not built yet, so renaming the secret today breaks every
# tool Lambda at cold start.
# WALLETS_SECRET_ID=xrpl-agentic-payments/wallets

# NOT WIRED UP. src/agents/orchestrator.py and src/mcp_server/server.py locate
# config/wallets.json relative to the repo root and ignore this. Kept because it
# names where they expect the file.
#
# When you would need it: running from a working directory other than the repo
# root, or pointing a local run at a second wallet file. The path override is not
# built yet — move or rename the file today and both modules simply fail to find
# it.
# WALLETS_FILE=config/wallets.json

# The two public XRPL addresses the web tier needs: the account the Execution
# Agent pays from, and the testnet RLUSD issuer it prices against. Both are
# public strings, which is the point — src/agents/orchestrator.py prefers these
# over config/wallets.json precisely so no pod has to mount a file full of
# seeds to recover them (see the resolution order at orchestrator.py:84). The
# EKS deployment sets both from CDK context; set them here for a local run
# against a deployment, or leave them blank and let provision_wallets.py's
# config/wallets.json supply them for local development.
XRPL_EXECUTION_ADDRESS=
XRPL_RLUSD_ISSUER=

# NOT WIRED UP, and easy to mistake for XRPL_RLUSD_ISSUER above, which IS read.
# The RLUSD_ISSUER constant in src/mcp_server/server.py and functions/shared.py
# comes from the wallet JSON's `_metadata.rlusd_issuer` — written by
# scripts/setup_trust_lines.py:197 — and never from the environment.
#
# When you would need it: pointing the tools at an issuer other than the one in
# the wallet file, i.e. a real RLUSD issuer instead of the treasury wallet this
# demo uses as a stand-in. The env override is not built yet.
# RLUSD_ISSUER=
